1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,162
Why can Python add five and two, but join
the text five and two into fifty two?

2
00:00:06,662 --> 00:00:10,087
Hi, I'm Mina, and this is Code in Small
Steps.

3
00:00:10,587 --> 00:00:18,571
In the next five minutes, you'll learn
what a variable is, the three basic types,
and how to fix the most common variable
error.

4
00:00:20,066 --> 00:00:24,236
This is lesson one. Variables: labelled
boxes for values.

5
00:00:24,736 --> 00:00:34,720
Here's the puzzle. Five plus two gives
seven. But five plus two, in quotes, gives
fifty two. Same symbol, different answer.

6
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:43,604
By the end, you'll be able to create a
variable, tell three types apart, predict
what plus does, and fix the most common
error.

7
00:00:44,104 --> 00:00:48,319
So, what is a variable? It's a name that
points to a value.

8
00:00:48,819 --> 00:00:54,420
When you write age equals eighteen, Python
stores the value eighteen, and puts the
label age on it.

9
00:00:54,920 --> 00:01:00,935
Think of a sticky note on a jar. The note
is the name, and whatever is inside the
jar is the value.

10
00:01:01,435 --> 00:01:05,564
The equals sign means store. It does not
mean, is equal to.

11
00:01:07,066 --> 00:01:13,106
Let's try it in the editor. I'll create
three variables: an age, a name, and a
price.

12
00:01:13,606 --> 00:01:18,119
Print them, and Python looks up each label
and shows its value.

13
00:01:18,619 --> 00:01:23,335
Now let's ask Python what type each value
is, using the type function.

14
00:01:23,835 --> 00:01:32,468
Eighteen is an int, a whole number. Mina,
in quotes, is a str, short for string. And
two point five is a float.

15
00:01:32,968 --> 00:01:36,869
Variables can also change. That's why
they're called variables.

16
00:01:37,369 --> 00:01:42,730
If I set age to nineteen and print it
again, the label age now points to the new
value.

17
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,895
One more thing before we move on: naming.

18
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:54,787
Names can use letters, numbers and
underscores, but they can't start with a
number, and they can't contain spaces.

19
00:01:55,287 --> 00:01:57,361
Total underscore score works fine.

20
00:01:57,861 --> 00:02:05,325
But if I replace the underscore with a
space and run it, Python can't read the
line, and stops with a syntax error.

21
00:02:05,825 --> 00:02:11,312
So stick to lowercase words joined by
underscores. That's the standard Python
style.

22
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:22,180
Those are the three basic types. Ints are
whole numbers. Floats have a decimal
point. And strings are text, always inside
quotes.

23
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:26,895
And here's the answer to our puzzle. The
type decides what plus does.

24
00:02:27,395 --> 00:02:32,548
With two ints, plus adds. With two
strings, plus joins the text.

25
00:02:33,048 --> 00:02:39,533
Mix a string and an int, and Python stops
with a type error, because it won't guess
what you meant.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,891
Let's prove it in a new file, with all
three cases side by side.

27
00:02:45,391 --> 00:02:45,829
Run it.

28
00:02:46,329 --> 00:02:52,320
Seven, then fifty two, and then a type
error on line three, exactly as predicted.

29
00:02:53,800 --> 00:03:00,722
To fix that, convert the type on purpose.
int of five gives the number five. str of
eighteen gives the text eighteen.

30
00:03:01,222 --> 00:03:06,132
This matters a lot, because anything a
user types into input arrives as a string.

31
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:14,057
So if someone types twelve, Python gives
you the text one two, and you convert it
with int before doing any maths.

32
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:18,046
Wrap the five in int, and run it again.

33
00:03:18,546 --> 00:03:20,634
Now the last line prints seven.

34
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:30,820
Your turn. Store two numbers in two
variables, and print their sum. Pause the
video, or try it during this ten second
timer.

35
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:47,086
Here's one solution. Twelve plus thirty
prints forty two.

36
00:03:47,586 --> 00:03:51,127
And with a comma, you can put the result
inside a sentence.

37
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:56,868
Finally, the most common error. Names must
match exactly.

38
00:03:57,368 --> 00:04:06,641
Here, score is spelled correctly on line
one, but misspelled on line two. Python
raises a name error, and even suggests the
right name.

39
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:09,423
Let's see it for real.

40
00:04:09,923 --> 00:04:16,375
Read the last line first. Name error, s c
o r is not defined. Did you mean, score?

41
00:04:16,875 --> 00:04:20,621
Fix the spelling, run again, and it prints
forty.

42
00:04:22,133 --> 00:04:26,034
Let's recap. A variable is a name that
points to a value.

43
00:04:26,534 --> 00:04:34,603
The type changes what operators like plus
do. And you can convert types on purpose,
with int, str and float.

44
00:04:35,103 --> 00:04:39,951
And whenever a result surprises you, check
the types with the type function.

45
00:04:41,466 --> 00:04:46,191
Next lesson, we'll use variables to make
decisions, with if and else.

46
00:04:46,691 --> 00:04:52,238
Before then, change the numbers in today's
code, and predict the result before you
run it.

47
00:04:52,738 --> 00:04:59,619
Thanks for learning with me today. If this
helped, subscribe, and leave a question in
the comments.

48
00:05:00,119 --> 00:05:02,002
See you in the next small step.
